  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "b1.media-and-news.trust-headline",
      "level": "B1",
      "topics": ["media-and-news"],
      "title": "Can you trust that headline?",
      "text": "Yesterday a surprising headline appeared on my phone: \"Chocolate is now healthier than fruit, scientists say.\" It was shared thousands of times in just a few hours. However, when I read the article carefully, I found out that the study was very small and that it was paid for by a chocolate company. The journalist didn't mention this until the last paragraph. Our media teacher told us that this happens a lot. She said that many people only read headlines and never check the source. According to a recent survey, more than half of teenagers get their news from social media, not from newspapers or TV. That's not always bad, but it means we have to be careful. Before I share a story now, I ask three questions: Who wrote it? When was it published? Do other news sites say the same thing? If the answer to the last question is no, it might be fake news.",
      "source": {
        "kind": "original"
      }
    }
  ],
